course: adult-life
lesson: 54
title: Reality TV: what is staged and what is real
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Reality TV is real people in made-up situations, filmed for weeks and cut down into dramatic episodes. The people are real and their feelings are real, but producers set up the situations, plan the twists, add the music and choose which few minutes you see.
explain: So a reality show is neither "all fake" nor "all true". It is real life, edited for drama, and the people in it go home afterwards to ordinary lives, where the comments people send them can really hurt.
explain: Vivid example: in a lounge in Auckland, Alfie watches young people in a villa shouting at each other. Dad pauses it: "They filmed about a hundred hours this week, mate. You're seeing forty minutes. Which bits do you reckon they picked?" Alfie grins: "The shouty ones."
explain: A good viewer enjoys the show and still remembers: the drama is chosen, the cameras are always on, and the contestants are real people who deserve the kindness you would show a classmate.
fact: Reality shows film real people for many hours and edit them into short episodes; producers choose the most dramatic moments and set up situations.
fact: A "diary room" looks private, but everything said there is filmed to be shown on TV; if there is a camera, it is not private.
fact: Talent shows choose and edit "sob stories" to make viewers care and vote; it is fair to vote for the best talent.
fact: Some judges play a "nasty" character because it makes good TV; honesty can be kind, and being honest does not mean being cruel.
fact: Tense music, close-ups and slow motion make ordinary mistakes feel dangerous; turn the sound down and the drama shrinks.
fact: Big "surprise" moments, like a makeover reveal, are sometimes filmed more than once; it can still be a happy day for the family.
fact: Jungle and survival shows have doctors, crews and safety rules behind the camera, even when the people look all alone.
fact: Bullying is still bullying when it is on TV and an audience cheers; reality stars are real people, and nasty messages online hurt them badly.
fact: Twists on reality shows are usually planned by producers to keep people watching, not lucky accidents.
fact: Most TV romances end after the show; real relationships take much longer than a few weeks of filming.
fact: Fame from reality TV is shiny but often short; it can bring no privacy and nasty comments, and most contestants go back to normal life.
fact: Makeover shows and influencers' "perfect" routines are often selling products; nobody needs a makeover to be worth something.
fact: People who did not agree to be filmed have their faces blurred; adults and children have a right to their privacy.
fact: Documentary shows about real hospitals film real patients who agreed; they show the exciting moments chosen from hundreds of hours.
exercise: Alfie thinks the people in the villa act like that all the time. What is really going on? | Real people in set-up situations, edited for drama | Actors reading lines from a film script written months before | A secret camera that nobody in the villa knows about | Real life filmed without any editing
exercise: A contestant cries in the "diary room". Is it a secret room where nobody is watching? | No; it is filmed so it can be shown on TV | Yes; nobody ever sees what happens in the diary room | Yes; only the contestant's mum can see it | No; it is where the judges sleep
exercise: A singer's sad life story is shown before she sings, and Jake votes because he feels sorry for her. What is fair? | Vote for whoever sings best | Always vote for the saddest story | Never vote for anyone with a sad story | Vote for whoever cries the most
exercise: A talent show judge is very rude to a contestant. Ruby says that's fine if you're honest. What does Mum say? | Honesty can be kind; the judge is playing a part | Being rude to people's faces is the best kind of honesty | The judge is right to be cruel to everyone | You should never tell anyone the truth
exercise: A cake collapses as the timer runs out, with loud scary music. Are the cooks in danger? | No; the music and close-ups make it feel tense | Yes; the oven is about to explode in the tent | Yes; losing the round means losing your house and your car | No; nothing really went wrong at all
exercise: A family is "surprised" by their new kitchen. Did it all happen in one go, exactly as shown? | Not always; the surprise is sometimes filmed more than once | Yes; TV cameras can only ever film anything once, so it was real | No; the family built the kitchen themselves | Yes; reality TV is never set up at all
exercise: People on a jungle show eat strange things. Are they really alone in the jungle? | No; doctors and crew are right behind the cameras | Yes; they must survive with nobody else around for miles | Yes; the camera films itself on a timer | No; they are in a studio in London
exercise: A contestant is picked on by the others and the audience cheers. Is it funny? | No; bullying is still bullying on TV | Yes; if an audience laughs, it is fine | Yes; it is only acting, so it doesn't count | No; but only because the show is boring
exercise: Theo learns a reality star got loads of nasty messages after the show. Why is he surprised? | He thought they were characters, not real people | He thought reality stars weren't allowed phones after the show | He thought nasty messages were against the law | He thought the show had not ended yet
exercise: Lily wants to know if the dating show couple lived happily ever after. What is most likely? | Most TV romances end after the show, though some last | All TV couples get married within a week of the final | They were never real people at all | They must stay together by contract for the rest of their lives
exercise: A twist comes out of nowhere on a reality show. Oscar thinks it was luck. What is more likely? | The producers planned it to keep people watching | A contestant's wish came true by some kind of magic | The weather caused it all by accident | The audience at home voted for it secretly
exercise: A family on a reality show has a huge row at dinner. Do they always argue like that? | No; the show picks the loudest few minutes | Yes; they argue every minute of every day, even in their sleep | Yes; families on TV never stop shouting | No; they are actors paid to argue once
exercise: Finn wants to be a reality star because it's easy and you get rich. What does Dad talk about? | The downsides: no privacy, nasty comments, fame that fades | That everyone who is on TV becomes a millionaire within a year | That reality stars never have to work again | That fame lasts forever once you have it
exercise: After a makeover show Ava thinks she needs a makeover to be beautiful. What does Mum say? | She is lovely as she is; the show is selling products | Yes; everyone needs a proper makeover by the age of ten | Yes; beauty only comes from hair and make-up | No; makeovers only work on famous people
exercise: On a police show some faces are blurred. Why? | Those people did not agree to be shown | The camera was broken just for those people | They are actors who forgot their make-up | They are the ones who did something bad
exercise: An influencer's "perfect" morning routine makes Mia feel her messy mornings are a failure. What is true? | Perfect routines are made for the camera | Everyone else's mornings really are perfect, every day | Her mornings are a failure and need fixing | Influencers never have to get up early
exercise: Isla is surprised the baking show winner gets a cake stand, not money. Why do people still enter? | The fame and the chances of jobs and book deals | Because they did not read the rules before entering | Because the cake stand is made of solid gold | Because they are forced to by the TV
exercise: Amelia asks if the patients on a hospital documentary are actors. What is true? | They are real patients who agreed to be filmed | They are all actors pretending to be ill for the cameras | They are filmed secretly without asking | They are doctors dressed up as patients
test: Mum, a nurse, laughs at the hospital show: "My shifts are tea, paperwork and quiet care. Nobody films that bit!" What does she mean? | Documentaries choose the exciting bits; ordinary days don't make TV | Her hospital is much more dramatic than the show, and they missed it all | The show made up all its patients | Nurses are not allowed to drink tea at work
test: Presenter, all smiles, as a contestant sobs on her way out: "Aww, see you next week!" George feels sorry for her. What is the kind view? | Noticing how the loser feels is a good habit, and the show looks after her | Losers deserve to cry, that's the game, and it's fun to watch them go | George is silly to care about people he only sees on TV | The presenter is laughing at her on purpose
test: Big brother: "Reality TV is literally all fake, they're actors innit." Put him right. | The people and feelings are real, but situations are set up and edited | He's right; every person on it is an actor from drama school | It is all true, with nothing edited out | They are robots made to look like people
test: Noah, worried: "They film naughty kids on that show, are they gonna film me?" What does Mum say? | Nobody will film him; children's privacy matters | Yes, if he misbehaves at the shops on a Saturday | Only if he is naughty three days in a row | Yes; every child is filmed once a year
test: Your mate: "That judge is savage, I'm gonna talk like that to people, it's just honest." What's the honest answer? | Being honest doesn't mean being cruel; the judge plays a part | Yes, rudeness is the bravest kind of honesty there is, so go for it | Only judges are allowed to tell the truth | Honesty means saying the worst thing you can
test: Theo's cousin posts under a reality star's photo: "ur so ugly get off telly." What does Dad say? | Don't send anyone a message you wouldn't say to their face | It doesn't matter; reality stars can't read | It's fine because she chose to be on TV, so she has to take it | Only post it if your friends agree
test: Dad mutes the cooking show at the tense bit. "Not so scary now, eh?" What has he shown? | Music makes ordinary moments feel dramatic | The cooks have stopped cooking now | Sound is what cooks the food on TV | The show was always silent anyway
test: Sophie's friend: "Everyone was cheering when they ganged up on her, so it's not bullying." What is true? | It's still bullying, even with cheering | If people cheer, it can't be bullying | It's only bullying in a school | It's fine if it's on TV at night
test: Lily, after the final: "They're getting married, they said so on the last show!" A year later they split. What's the lesson? | Real relationships take longer than a few weeks on TV | TV couples always split after exactly one year, it's a rule | The show lied about their names too | They should have stayed on TV longer
test: Grace: "She told the diary room a secret, so nobody knows!" What's wrong with that? | The diary room is filmed for millions to see | Diary rooms only film in the dark, so nothing shows | Secrets told on TV are kept by law | The cameras there are just for show
test: Finn, dreamy: "Reality star, then I'm rich and famous forever." Dad: "Hmm." What is Dad thinking of? | Most go back to normal life, and fame has a hard side | Every reality star buys a mansion with the money | Only children can be reality stars | Fame is guaranteed for life after one show on TV
test: Poppy's nan sniffs: "That makeover reveal was fake, they did it three times." Is the show then bad? | Not necessarily; a set-up moment can still be a happy day for the family | Yes; anything filmed twice is a lie and the whole show is a cheat | Yes; the family must have hated the house | No, because Nan is always wrong
exam: Identify the principal manner in which reality television programmes are produced. | Real participants are filmed at length and edited for dramatic effect | Professional actors perform from a fully written script | Events are broadcast in their entirety without editing | Participants are filmed without their knowledge or consent
exam: Whereas a participant speaks within a so-called diary room, determine the status of the remarks so made. | They are recorded with a view to public broadcast | They remain confidential to the participant alone | They are disclosed solely to the participant's family | They are destroyed upon the conclusion of filming
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established understanding of the conduct of a television judge who habitually disparages contestants. | The judge frequently adopts a role for entertainment | The judge is obliged by law to be discourteous to contestants | The judge's cruelty is the only form of honesty | The judge is selected at random from the audience
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the faces of certain persons are obscured in documentary programmes concerning the police. | Those persons have not consented to being shown | Those persons are professional actors in disguise | Those persons have been convicted of criminal offences | The camera malfunctioned in their presence
